    <h1>Why Choose a Small VPN Over NordVPN or ExpressVPN</h1>
    <p class="article-intro">NordVPN, ExpressVPN, Surfshark — these names are everywhere. Millions of subscribers, astronomical marketing budgets, influencers promoting them in every video. But does that make them the best choice? Not necessarily.</p>

    <h2>The Overselling Problem</h2>
    <p>Big VPNs work like low-cost airlines: they sell more seats than they actually have. Thousands of users share the same servers simultaneously. The result: during peak hours, connections slow down, latency spikes, and you're paying for a degraded service.</p>
    <p>This is the main reason many people complain their VPN "slows everything down." It's not the VPN itself — it's server overload from sharing resources with thousands of others.</p>

    <h2>What VulcainVPN Does Differently</h2>
    <p>VulcainVPN is an independent service with a <strong>dedicated server in France and a deliberately limited subscriber count</strong>. No overselling. Server resources aren't shared with thousands of strangers.</p>
    <p>The direct impact on speed: <strong>if your base internet connection is fast, your VPN connection will be too</strong>. Fibre at 500 Mb/s? You'll keep most of that speed through the VPN. This is fundamentally different from a service where your speed depends on how many other users are connected at the same time.</p>

    <div class="article-highlight">
      <h3>Big VPN vs VulcainVPN</h3>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Servers</strong> — Thousands of shared servers vs <strong>dedicated France server</strong></p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Users per server</strong> — Thousands vs <strong>deliberately limited</strong></p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Speed</strong> — Variable based on load vs <strong>close to your base speed</strong></p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Transparency</strong> — Marketing policy vs <strong>detailed published log policy</strong></p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Price</strong> — €3-10/month with long commitment vs <strong>€15/month no mandatory annual lock-in</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h2>The Marketing Question</h2>
    <p>NordVPN spends tens of millions per year on advertising and sponsorships. That budget comes from somewhere — your subscriptions. VulcainVPN has no such marketing budget. What you pay goes into infrastructure and service, not YouTube sponsorships.</p>

    <h2>Do Big VPNs Have Real Advantages?</h2>
    <p>Yes, to be honest:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Server count and countries</strong> — if you need to appear from Australia, Japan or Brazil, a big VPN with hundreds of locations is more suitable</li>
      <li><strong>Polished apps</strong> — years of development with large teams</li>
      <li><strong>Independent audits</strong> — some major VPNs have had their infrastructure audited by third parties</li>
    </ul>
    <p>VulcainVPN is optimised for a specific use case: <strong>a fast, private, reliable connection from or to France</strong>. For that need, a well-managed dedicated server beats an overloaded network of thousands of shared servers.</p>

    <h2>Transparency as a Differentiator</h2>
    <p>Big VPNs publish "no logs" policies but stay vague on details. VulcainVPN publishes exactly what it collects, why, and for how long — including connection metadata retained for 24-48h for the device limit. <a href="<%= logging_path %>">Read the full policy</a>.</p>

